C2 — Go scanning bites on the brief's stated plant: FAIL
  The brief's exact plant is NOT caught.
  cmd: appended to internal/templates/render.go:
         func plantA() string { x := "text-fuchsia-333"; return x }
       bash swarm/t7-coverage.sh
  out: "OK: 732 literal utility-looking class tokens covered ..."  exit: 0
       (expected: non-zero listing text-fuchsia-333)
  Cause (script lines 262-287, 338-365): a bare string literal is harvested
  ONLY when its enclosing identifier is "class-shaped" -- is_class_ident()
  requires exactly class/className, or a name ENDING in Class/Classes/
  ClassName. `x` matches neither, so `x := "..."` is never examined. This is a
  deliberate, documented scoping choice by the worker, but it is not what the
  criterion states, and X-RUN-SPEC.md X4 likewise says "show a planted fake
  utility class in a Go literal in the copy IS caught" -- a bare `x := "..."`
  is a Go literal and is not caught.

  What DOES bite (same file, same token shape, each reverted after):
    plant `return `<div class="text-fuchsia-333">x</div>`` -> exit 1,
      "UNCOVERED ... - text-fuchsia-333"
    plant `class := "text-fuchsia-444"`                     -> exit 1,
      "UNCOVERED ... - text-fuchsia-444"
    plant `func plantColorClass() string { return "text-fuchsia-555" }`
                                                            -> exit 1,
      "UNCOVERED ... - text-fuchsia-555"
    revert all -> "OK: 732 ..." exit 0
  Mitigating evidence for the lead's adjudication (does the scoping hide a
  REAL hole today?): a strict Tailwind-shape sweep of every string literal in
  every production .go file under internal/ and cmd/ found 17 genuinely
  Tailwind-shaped tokens (bg-red-50, border-red-200, dark:bg-red-900/30,
  dark:border-red-800, dark:text-red-300, dark:text-red-400, font-medium, h-5,
  mr-2, mt-2, p-4, rounded-lg, text-red-500, text-red-600, text-red-700,
  text-sm, w-5) and ALL 17 appear in the script's harvested candidate set
  (checked against an instrumented dump of @candidates, 780 entries). So the
  narrowed extraction produces no actual coverage hole in this repo as it
  stands; the gap is between the implementation and the criterion as written,
  not a demonstrated missed class.

